WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.366
An MCP architecture usually starts with a host application.

00:00:03.366 --> 00:00:09.172
The host is the AI application or work environment where the user interacts with the model.

00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:10.986
Inside or alongside the host,

00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:14.615
an MCP client communicates with one or more MCP servers.

00:00:14.615 --> 00:00:18.243
The server exposes capabilities such as tools, resources, or prompts.

00:00:18.243 --> 00:00:22.961
Those capabilities may connect to downstream systems such as local files, cloud services,

00:00:22.961 --> 00:00:26.589
developer platforms, ticketing systems, databases, monitoring tools, or internal APIs.

00:00:26.589 --> 00:00:30.581
The host, client, and server roles matter because they separate responsibilities.

00:00:30.581 --> 00:00:35.661
The host decides how the user experience works and how model interaction is orchestrated.

00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:38.927
The client handles the MCP connection to a server.

00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:44.370
The server describes available capabilities and carries out requests according to its implementation and policy.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.724
This separation helps an AI application use external systems without embedding every

00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:50.901
integration detail directly into the host.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:53.441
MCP servers can be local or remote.

00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:57.796
A local server may run on the same machine as the host

00:00:57.796 --> 00:00:59.973
and expose local tools or files.

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:05.416
A remote server may run as a network service and connect to a cloud platform,

00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:08.319
SaaS product, enterprise API, or shared data source.

00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:11.222
Local and remote models create different operational concerns.

00:01:11.222 --> 00:01:14.488
Local servers may raise workstation access and execution questions.

00:01:14.488 --> 00:01:17.754
Remote servers may raise authentication, transport, tenant isolation, availability,

00:01:17.754 --> 00:01:19.205
and vendor governance questions.

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:22.108
Identity flows through this architecture in several ways.

00:01:22.108 --> 00:01:25.737
A user identity may represent the person making the request.

00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:28.277
An application identity may represent the host.

00:01:28.277 --> 00:01:31.905
A service identity may represent the server or downstream integration.

00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:34.445
The model may propose a tool call,

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:37.348
but it should not become the permission system.

00:01:37.348 --> 00:01:40.977
MCP helps separate AI application behavior from external system integration,

00:01:40.977 --> 00:01:44.243
but teams still need to design authorization, consent, logging,

00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:46.420
and ownership around the full path.
